  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 101 AM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThursday is forecast to start the period with the area under \nnorthwest flow aloft. With high pressure at the surface, \ntemperatures should either be slightly cooler in the 70s, or similar \nto Wednesday in the low 80s. Plenty of sunshine and winds around 5 \nto 15 mph are forecast underneath the high.\n\nFriday, an upper trough is forecast to push into the Northwestern \nUnited States with some slight ridging ahead in the Plains. This \nshould lead to a slightly warmer day with highs likely in the 80s. \nWith low pressure deepening ahead of the upper trough over the High \nPlains, winds should be a bit stronger around 15-25 mph with gusts \nto 35 mph. May have to keep an eye out for critical fire weather \nconditions near the Tri-State border as drier air tries to push in \nfrom the west. However, the air is forecast to already be dry enough \nthat we shouldn't see precipitation, even with the advancing \nfeatures. Saturday should be fairly similar to Friday with the upper \ntrough forecast to stall over the High Plains while deepening in the \nWestern United States. If temperatures are a bit warmer though, \ncritical fire weather conditions could develop for counties along \nthe Colorado border with RH dipping more into the teens.\n\nSunday and beyond gets a little less clear as there is a fair amount \nof spread in how deep the upper trough becomes and how quickly it \npushes through the area. For now, the forecast has the area cooling \ngoing into the beginning of the week with a cold front pushing \nthrough. Precipitation chances are also forecast to increase with \nmid to high level moisture being pulled in from the southwest ahead \nof the upper trough. If precipitation did occur, it would likely be \nrain and fairly light with dry low levels.\n\nThat all being said, the GEFS and ECMWF ENS both show fairly equal \nchances for a slower and faster trough progression. The slower \nprogression could lead increased precipitation chances with more \ntime to pull in gulf moisture and have a low sit over the area. \nSevere weather chances would also be possible. On the flip side, the \nslower trough would give the area much cooler temperatures. There is \neven a 10% chance for freezing temperatures and snow. While both of \nthese scenarios would likely be more impactful, for now am sticking \nwith a more median solution which seems to verify more often in \nsituations like this. We'll have to watch and see how this next big \nsystem develops.",
